{"schemaVersion":"1.0.0","commitSha":"c2534ff9c84422e00711da31629a76f3f59feb98","shard":"6f5fc177a75cdd0a","symbols":[{"path":"xrpl/Workers","kind":"class","name":"Workers","qualifiedName":"xrpl::Workers","scope":"xrpl","category":"production","access":"public","documentation":{"status":"present","brief":[{"type":"code","text":"Workers"},{"type":"text","text":" is effectively a thread pool."}],"description":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The constructor takes a \"callback\" that has a "},{"type":"code","text":"void processTask(int instance)"},{"type":"text","text":" method, and a number of workers. It creates that many "},{"type":"code","text":"Worker"},{"type":"text","text":"s and then waits for calls to "},{"type":"code","text":"Workers::addTask()"},{"type":"text","text":". It holds a semaphore that counts the number of pending \"tasks\", and a condition variable for the event when the last worker pauses itself."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A \"task\" is just a call to the callback's "},{"type":"code","text":"processTask"},{"type":"text","text":" method. \"Adding a task\" means calling that method now, or remembering to call it in the future. This is implemented with a semaphore. If there are any workers waiting when a task is added, then one will be woken to claim the task. If not, then the next worker to wait on the semaphore will claim the task."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Creating a "},{"type":"code","text":"Worker"},{"type":"text","text":" creates a thread that calls "},{"type":"code","text":"Worker::run()"},{"type":"text","text":". When that thread enters "},{"type":"code","text":"Worker::run"},{"type":"text","text":", it increments the count of active workers in the parent "},{"type":"code","text":"Workers"},{"type":"text","text":" object and then tries to claim a task, which blocks if there are none pending. It will be unblocked whenever the semaphore is notified (i.e. when the number of pending tasks is incremented). That only happens in two circumstances: (1) when "},{"type":"code","text":"Workers::addTask"},{"type":"text","text":" is called and (2) when "},{"type":"code","text":"Workers"},{"type":"text","text":" wants to pause some workers (\"pause one worker\" is considered one task), which happens when someone wants to stop the workers or shrink the threadpool. No worker threads are ever destroyed until "},{"type":"code","text":"Workers"},{"type":"text","text":" is destroyed; it merely pauses workers until then."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"When a waiting worker is woken, it checks whether "},{"type":"code","text":"Workers"},{"type":"text","text":" is trying to pause workers. If so, it changes its status from active to paused and blocks on its own condition variable. If not, then it calls "},{"type":"code","text":"processTask"},{"type":"text","text":" on the \"callback\" held by "},{"type":"code","text":"Workers"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"When a paused worker is woken, it checks whether it should exit. The signal to exit is only set in the destructor of "},{"type":"code","text":"Worker"},{"type":"text","text":", which unblocks the paused thread and waits for it to exit. A "},{"type":"code","text":"Worker::run"},{"type":"text","text":" thread checks whether it needs to exit only when it is woken from a pause (not when it is woken from waiting). This is why the destructor for "},{"type":"code","text":"Workers"},{"type":"text","text":" pauses all the workers before destroying them."}]}],"returns":null,"notes":[],"suppressionReason":null,"inheritedFrom":null},"location":{"declaration":{"file":"include/xrpl/core/detail/Workers.h","line":60},"definition":{"file":"include/xrpl/core/detail/Workers.h","line":61,"endLine":217}},"corrections":[],"templateParameters":null,"isTemplateSpecialization":false,"isFinal":false,"isAbstract":false,"baseClasses":[],"